#
# First-party bootstrap image for the chart's TLS hooks (internal-tls-job, webhook-cert-job).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Those hooks do two things: self-sign an internal CA (needs `openssl`) and talk to
# the Kubernetes API to patch the webhook caBundle / write the TLS Secret. The previous image,
# alpine/k8s, ships kubectl but NOT openssl — verified, not assumed:
#
#     docker run --entrypoint /bin/sh alpine/k8s:1.30.0 -c 'which openssl'   ->  not found
#
# so the hooks fell back to `apk add --no-cache openssl` at runtime, which requires writing the
# package DB, which requires root. That `runAsUser: 0` is rejected outright by OpenShift's default
# restricted-v2 SCC, so `helm install` died at the pre-install hook — nothing deployed at all.
#
# Baking openssl in removes the runtime `apk add`, and with it the only reason these hooks needed
# root. It also removes a network dependency from the install path: a hook that apk-adds at runtime
# fails in an air-gapped or egress-restricted cluster, which is a common posture for the sort of
# environment that runs a policy-enforcement product.
#
# THIS IMAGE DOES NOT CONTAIN kubectl — see the NO-kubectl note below before writing a hook that
# assumes it does. The API calls are made with `curl` and the pod's service-account token.
#
# OPENSHIFT / ARBITRARY UID. OpenShift assigns a random UID from the namespace's allocated range
# (typically ~1000650000+) with GID 0. An image that only works as one specific UID breaks there. So:
# nothing here is owned by a fixed user, the working directory is group-writable by GID 0, and the
# default USER is numeric (not a name) so `runAsNonRoot: true` can verify it without a lookup.
# The chart omits runAsUser entirely when openshift.enabled=true, letting the platform assign.

ARG ALPINE_VERSION=3.21
FROM alpine:${ALPINE_VERSION}

# openssl: self-signs the internal CA. curl + ca-certificates: talks to the Kubernetes API directly.
#
# NO kubectl. It was here originally, and the fail-closed trivy gate in build.yml refused to publish
# the image because kubectl vendors a golang.org/x/net with fixable HIGH/CRITICAL CVEs — and bumping
# v1.31.4 -> v1.36.3 did not clear them, because upstream ships what it ships. Baking a third-party Go
# binary we cannot patch into a FIRST-PARTY image pulled it into scope of our own scanner (the runtime
# `bitnami/kubectl` reference used elsewhere was never scanned by us, which is why this had not bitten
# before).
#
# The hooks only ever did three things — GET/PATCH a MutatingWebhookConfiguration and apply a Secret —
# all plain authenticated REST calls. curl with the pod's service-account token does them with no Go
# runtime in the image at all, so there is nothing here for the scanner to find.
# jq: the uninstall finalizer hook lists NrvqPolicy/NrvqClass/NrvqConfig and strips their finalizers,
# which means reading namespace/name out of a Kubernetes list response. That hook previously used
# `bitnami/kubectl:1.31`, and Bitnami REMOVED those tags from Docker Hub in 2025 — the image 404s, the
# pod sits in ImagePullBackOff, and `helm uninstall` fails on a deadline. Worse, that hook is what
# releases the `norviq.io/policy-protection` finalizers, so its failure strands every CR in
# Terminating; a later REINSTALL then adopts the tombstones and the controller loops on a delete the
# API rejects, leaving the cluster blocking every tool call on `no_policy_loaded`.
#
# Moving the hook onto this first-party image removes the whole failure class: no third-party tag that
# can vanish, and the same digest-pinned, scanned image the TLS hooks already use. jq is ~1MB from
# Alpine main — a C binary, so unlike kubectl it brings no vendored Go module tree for the trivy gate
# to reject. Parsing with sed/grep instead was the alternative and is exactly the kind of fragile
# string-matching that breaks silently on an unexpected field.
RUN apk add --no-cache ca-certificates openssl curl jq \
    && rm -rf /var/cache/apk/*


# A writable HOME and working directory. openssl writes the CA key/cert here, and the hooks run
# against a read-only root filesystem, so this must be somewhere the runtime user can write. Under
# OpenShift the runtime UID is unpredictable but the GID is always 0, so these are group-writable
# rather than owned by any particular user. Callers still mount a tmpfs over it (the chart's
# read-only-rootfs contract); this is the fallback that keeps the image usable if they don't.
#
# No KUBECACHEDIR: that existed only for kubectl's discovery cache, and kubectl is not in this image
# (see above). The chart's uninstall-finalizer Job still sets it, but that Job runs a THIRD-PARTY
# bitnami/kubectl image, not this one.
RUN mkdir -p /workspace \
    && chgrp -R 0 /workspace \
    && chmod -R g=u /workspace
WORKDIR /workspace
ENV HOME=/workspace

# Numeric so `runAsNonRoot: true` can enforce it without resolving a name. OpenShift overrides this
# with its own allocated UID, which still lands in GID 0 and so retains access to /workspace.
USER 65532:0

ENTRYPOINT ["/bin/sh"]
